:"dark ocean"}],"gloss":"dark ocean"},{"surface":"உடுத்த","parts":[{"text":"உடுத்த","lemma":"உடு","gloss":"clothed/girdled"}],"gloss":"girdled"},{"surface":"இவ்","parts":[{"text":"இவ்","lemma":"இ","gloss":"this"}],"gloss":"this"},{"surface":"வையகத்து","parts":[{"text":"வையகத்து","lemma":"வையகம்","gloss":"world (oblique)"}],"gloss":"world's"},{"surface":"அருந்திறல்","parts":[{"text":"அருந்திறல்","lemma":"அருந்திறல்","gloss":"rare strength"}],"gloss":"rare strength"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"பொன்படு","parts":[{"text":"பொன்","lemma":"பொன்","gloss":"gold"},{"text":"படு","lemma":"படு","gloss":"producing"}],"gloss":"gold-producing"},{"surface":"மால்வரை","parts":[{"text":"மால்","lemma":"மால்","gloss":"great"},{"text":"வரை","lemma":"வரை","gloss":"mountain"}],"gloss":"great mountain"},{"surface":"கிழவ","parts":[{"text":"கிழவ","lemma":"கிழவன்","gloss":"lord (vocative)"}],"gloss":"O lord"},{"surface":"வென்வேல்","parts":[{"text":"வென்வேல்","lemma":"வென்வேல்","gloss":"victorious spear"}],"gloss":"victorious spear"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"உடலுநர்","parts":[{"text":"உடலுநர்","lemma":"உடலுநர்","gloss":"enemies"}],"gloss":"enemies"},{"surface":"உட்கும்","parts":[{"text":"உட்கும்","lemma":"உட்கு","gloss":"fearing"}],"gloss":"fearing"},{"surface":"தானை","parts":[{"text":"தானை","lemma":"தானை","gloss":"army"}],"gloss":"army"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"கெடல்அரும்","parts":[{"text":"கெடல்அரும்","lemma":"கெடல்அரும்","gloss":"hard to ruin"}],"gloss":"hard to ruin"},{"surface":"குறைய","parts":[{"text":"குறைய","lemma":"குறைய","gloss":"request"}],"gloss":"request","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"நாடுகிழவோயே","parts":[{"text":"நாடு","lemma":"நாடு","gloss":"country"},{"text":"கிழவோய்","lemma":"கிழவோன்","gloss":"lord"},{"text":"ஏ","lemma":"ஏ","gloss":"emphasis"}],"gloss":"O lord of the country"}]}],"literal":"If you ask, \"Who are these people?\" / Along with towns, to suppliants giving, along with chariots, / to the jasmine vine gave, unfading good fame, / having sounding-bell elephants, Parampu's lord, / tall great Pari's daughters [they are]; and I, / their father's friend; these are my daughters; / a Brahmin, a poet, having taken [them], I came. / You, appearing from the sacrificial pot of the northern sage, / having fashioned with copper, constructed high tall fortress walls, / with unceasing generosity, Dvaraka having ruled, / forty-nine generations having come, / among Velir chiefs, the Vel chief! Victorious in war, lord! / Wearing garlands, having elephants, great dark Irungovel! / Because of possessing manly duty, fulfilling the bards' duty, / wearing a sprouting chaplet, tiger-chasing great one! / As I give, please accept these women; sky-covering / dark ocean girdled this world's rare strength, / gold-producing great mountain's lord! Victorious spear / that makes enemies fear, having army, / having a request hard to ruin, lord of the country!","translation":"If you ask, 'Who are these girls?' they are the daughters of the great Pari, the lord of Parampu hill where elephants with ringing bells roam—the one of unfading fame who gave away his towns to suppliants and his chariot to a jasmine vine. As for me, I am his friend, a Brahmin poet, and I have brought them here as my own daughters. You are the great Irungovel, who arose from the sacrificial pot of the northern sage, whose ancestors ruled Dvaraka with its high copper-girdled walls and unceasing generosity for forty-nine generations. O victorious lord of wars, who wears garlands and commands elephants! O great one who chased away the tiger, wearing a fresh chaplet, fulfilling your noble duty to bards! I offer these girls to you; please accept them in marriage, O lord of the gold-bearing mountains, ruler of this vast land girdled by the dark ocean, who commands a fearsome army and whose requests are never denied!","modern":"இவர்கள் யார் என்று நீ கேட்டால், ஒலிக்கின்ற மணிகளைக் கொண்ட யானைகளையும் பறம்பு மலையையும் உடைய தலைவனான, இரவலர்களுக்கு ஊர்களையும் முல்லைக் கொடிக்குத் தேரையும் கொடுத்து அழியாத புகழைப் பெற்ற பாரியின் மகள்கள் இவர்கள். நான் அவனுடைய தோழனாகிய அந்தணப் புலவன்; இவர்களை என் மகள்களாகக் கொண்டு வந்துள்ளேன். நீயோ, வடதிசை முனிவரின் வேள்விக் குண்டத்தில் தோன்றி, செம்பினால் இயற்றப்பட்ட உயர்ந்த மதில்களைக் கொண்ட துவாரகையை ஆண்டு, நாற்பத்தொன்பது தலைமுறைகளாக வந்த வேளிர்குலத் தலைவன். மாலை அணிந்த யானைகளை உடைய இருங்கோவேளே! பாணர்களின் வறுமையைப் போக்கும் கடமையைச் செய்யும், தழைத்த மாலையை அணிந்த, புலியை வென்ற மாலே! நான் இவர்களை உனக்குத் தருகிறேன், நீ ஏற்றுக் கொள்வாயாக. கடல் சூழ்ந்த இவ்வுலகில் பொன் விளையும் பெரிய மலைக்குத் தலைவனே! பகைவர் அஞ்சும் படையையும், மறுக்க முடியாத வேண்டுகோளையும் உடைய நாட்டின் தலைவனே!","explanation":"This poem is spoken by the famous poet Kapilar to the Velir chief Irungovel. Following the tragic death of his close friend and patron, King Pari of Parampu, Kapilar took charge of Pari's unmarried daughters. He travels to various chiefs to find suitable grooms for them, and here he presents them to Irungovel. Kapilar praises Pari's legendary generosity, reminding the chief of the girls' noble lineage, and then extols Irungovel's own illustrious ancestry, tracing it back forty-nine generations to Dvaraka and a sage's sacrificial fire. He appeals to Irungovel's sense of honor and duty to accept the daughters in marriage. The poem belongs to the pāṭāṇ tiṇai as it praises the lineage and virtues of the chief while presenting a request.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"}}